# TODO - Multi-objective compiler algorithm evaluation arena

## Objective

Evaluate compiler and execution algorithms over scalable challenge families and
produce capacity curves and Pareto frontiers rather than one pass/fail score.
Measure time-to-verified-solution, generated-code size, runtime instructions,
peak memory/VRAM, verifier cost, energy or device utilization when available,
success probability for stochastic methods, and maximum solved difficulty under
fixed budgets. Preserve raw evidence so an algorithm that is faster at easy
instances but scales worse than another remains distinguishable instead of both
appearing equally "perfect" after crossing an arbitrary threshold.

## Acceptance

- Authoritative comparisons retain capacity curves and Pareto frontiers; an
  aggregate score, if offered, is explicitly secondary and cannot hide
  time/quality/memory/size/verifier trade-offs.
- The arena measures every available execution/compiler stage without making
  a later target backend a prerequisite for the evaluation infrastructure; final
  `.malbolge` measurements are added when verified artifacts become available.
- The work states a falsifiable question or hypothesis, an explicit baseline,
  and an observation that would reject or materially weaken the proposed
  technique before performance conclusions are accepted.
- If executable algorithm research is required, the stable ID is mirrored under
  `docs/research/algorithms/<id>/` and `algorithms/<id>/`; ordinary product
  engineering is not forced into that mirror.
- Performance conclusions use equivalent workloads and report raw-sample
  provenance, resource budgets, dispersion/uncertainty, and failure/success
  behavior rather than only a best-case number.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `benchmarks/arena/`,
  `docs/research/methodology/`, `algorithms/`.
- `benchmarks/arena/analyze.py` now admits version-one reported trial records,
  matches algorithms on exact challenge seed/hash/trial cohorts, retains failure
  rates, and computes conditional-cost Pareto sets and capacity curves with
  exact integer-rational medians, reported fixed-budget overage rejection, and
  no fabricated solved thresholds. Exact median absolute deviations and
  observed ranges describe successful costs; a separate all-attempt elapsed
  summary preserves failure costs. Named verifiers must agree per matched case.
  A secondary paired baseline view retains both-success and asymmetric failure
  outcomes, plus signed per-cost differences only for mutually verified trials.
  Synthetic-only regressions live under `benchmarks/arena/tests/`.
- An optional `benchmarks/arena/evidence.py` entrypoint now cross-checks the
  reported algorithm, challenge, profile, host/toolchain, oracle, workload,
  outcome, and raw path against the governed experiment run manifest parser.
  It admits only existing nonempty regular raw files and bounded manifests;
  synthetic regressions reject mismatches, unsafe links, and missing files.
- Opt-in `arena-raw-v1` CSV checking now binds every retained trial row and
  exact metric to its JSON report, including failure outcomes. The analyzer
  returns inspected raw-byte SHA-256 fingerprints and rejects mismatches,
  missing rows, duplicates and malformed CSV under a bounded input limit.
- A strict opt-in version-one `arena_trial` manifest extension now binds a
  unique recorded run to each repetition index and retained raw-file SHA-256.
  This mode implies complete `arena-raw-v1` row checks, rejects aggregate-run
  reuse, and preserves older aggregate-manifest workflows without pretending
  that a producer-reported digest or outcome is independently certified.
- A separate opt-in budget link requires every reported run's whole-second
  and MiB budget declarations to agree exactly with the arena study's
  nanosecond and byte limits, rejecting stale or unequal research budgets.
  This is not evidence of actual resource monitoring or enforcement.
- A package-local BasedPyright project now checks the independent arena
  implementation and test files without modifying repository-wide tooling
  selection; the imported historical CSV header is typed as a sequence.
- An optional exact `energy_uj` cost and version-two `arena-raw-v2` CSV
  admit microjoule observations without changing v1 bytes, rejecting lost
  energy fields or inconsistent trial costs. No real energy measurements,
  calibration or physical consumption claim is yet available.
- An optional bounded challenge-bundle preflight now regenerates original
  parametric C source, binary oracle and canonical manifest bytes in memory
  and matches every reported workload digest to the regenerated C source.
  This confirms generator provenance only, not guest verifier replay or an
  observed benchmark run.
- Optional same-accelerator admission retains per-run declared device
  identities and rejects mixed cohorts where a controlled same-device
  comparison is required; device declarations remain source-reported, not
  hardware attestation or utilization measurement.
- A synthetic all-checks integration fixture combines generated challenge
  provenance with versioned energy CSV, raw hashes, individual trial runs,
  exact budget agreement and matched accelerator labels. It proves admission
  consistency, not source execution or independent verification.
- The analyzer is not an independent verifier. Even a matching raw CSV is
  only a producer report, not trusted measurement or verifier replay evidence.
  Required next evidence includes protocol-validated run manifests with real
  verifier outcomes, raw multidimensional samples, budget enforcement, and
  published stage comparisons
  on reproducible real challenges. Later verified `.malbolge` measurements
  remain dependent on the final compiler backend.
- A read-only `benchmarks/arena/historical_pairs.py` adapter now projects two
  existing classic September 4, 2026 paired timing records through the arena.
  It links governed run/protocol identities, complete paired trials, registered
  quality maps, structural verifier counts, and a matching retained source
  commit pin, with exact source byte fingerprints.
  The original research records remain the evidence authority; no new runs,
  verifier replay, or multi-difficulty scaling conclusion is asserted.
- Required evidence: reproducible run commands, retained challenge and
  algorithm identities, raw multidimensional samples, and independent verifier
  outcomes for every measured stage.
- Prerequisite completion evidence: `parametric-compiler-challenge-generator`,
  `benchmark-and-statistical-evidence-protocol`,
  `reproducible-experiment-identity-and-manifest`.
- Research evidence pending: bibliography-backed context, experiment identity,
  reproducible configuration, retained negative/null results, and a reviewed
  conclusion with threats to validity.
- Performance evidence pending: raw measurements plus a reproducible
  scaling/statistical summary tied to exact workload and hardware/software
  identity.
